frac{1}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501168 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501168 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501168 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-6x+10)$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bc52b75-4c42-58c5-bcab-077cbe6faec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501168 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-6x+10) & =-({(x-3)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501188 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501188 