en, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+6x) & =4({(x+3)}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-36\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501206 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501206 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-3x+1)$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aef8db66-e528-55a5-877b-5de5c3121fc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501206 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501218 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501218 #edb/ref 